// The sprite draw path, located by breaking on it (T11k).
attr[seg_0e97:038b]: type = code; name = draw_sprite; comment = [[[
    Draws one sprite. Called 445 times during a boot -- the hot graphics routine. DS:SI arrives pointing at the sprite's 8-byte header, normalised so SI is 8 to 12; observed headers 48x48, 32x20 and 16x1, matching the width-1/height-1 layout of FORMATS 3.7. Callers seen: 0x0534 and 0x055e.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:02dd]: type = code; name = draw_sprite_variant; comment = [[[
    Runs 443 times, paired with draw_sprite; the two are almost certainly the flipped and unflipped paths selected by bit 0 of the descriptor byte at +0x22.
]]]

attr[seg_0e97:0398]: type = code; name = sprite_x_halved; comment = [[[
    shr ax,1 on the x offset. With the shr at 0x03f7 on the row offset this is what
    establishes that sprite pixels are 4bpp, two per byte (FORMATS 3.10). Every earlier
    render assumed 8bpp, which is why they all sheared.
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:03f1]: type = code; name = sprite_row_offset; comment = [[[
    mov bx,[si+2] / inc bx / mul bx / shr ax,1 -- source offset for row y is
    y * width / 2, so a row is ceil(width/2) bytes. Sprites chain end to end:
    record size = 8 + ceil(width/2) * height, confirmed against 30 sprites read out of
    memory here (FORMATS 3.10, T11k).
]]]
attr[seg_0e97:0463]: type = code; name = sprite_src_advance; comment = [[[
    add si,8 -- skips the 8-byte header, so pixel data starts at header+8.
    The following les di,ss:[1dbf] / mul ss:[1dd5] computes the destination in the
    framebuffer from ss:[0c2c] (x) and ss:[0c2e] (y).
]]]

end
